<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Luis Potosí</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Luis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> is a state of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, with a capital of the same name founded in 1592 after the discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> nearby. Its eastern part lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a>, home to Huastec and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> communities. The state was an important mining and transport centre in colonial and modern times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi.jpg" alt="San Luis Potosí" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucy Nieto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerro_del_Fraile_-_Matehuala,_San_Luis_Potos%C3%AD,_M%C3%A9xico.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>San Luis Potosí, officially the Free and Sovereign State of San Luis Potosí, is one of the 32 federal entities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and takes its name from its capital. It lies in eastern and central Mexico and is divided into 59 municipalities. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, Hidalgo, Querétaro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>. The state lies mostly on the Mexican Plateau, at a mean elevation of about 1,800 metres, while the Sierra Madre Oriental separates the plateau from the Gulf Coastal Plain to the east. The eastern quarter is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huasteca">Huasteca</a> potosina, part of a region along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> historically associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huastec-people">Huastec people</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In pre-Columbian times the territory contained parts of the cultural areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> and Aridoamerica. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a>, nomadic hunter-gatherers, lived in the north and the west-central parts. Many indigenous people died during Spanish colonisation, but Huasteco groups still live in the state, together with Pame and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> peoples.</p><p>The discovery of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> in 1592 led to the founding of the first town, known as San Luis de Mezquitique, at the site of the present capital, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> was appointed first mayor. The name San Luis honours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> compares the wealth of the place with the rich silver mines of Potosí in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, a rivalry the settlers hoped for but never fully achieved. In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, Augustinians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> built churches and other buildings, many still standing.</p><p>In mid-1821, after Mexican independence, General José Antonio Echávarri pressed the mayor and city council to surrender the city to the Army of the Three Guarantees, and independence was proclaimed in San Luis Potosí. The first state constitution was written on 16 October 1826 and remained in effect until 1835, when Congress proclaimed a centralist system. The state's part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> of 1846–1847 earned it the name San Luis de la Patria. During the French Intervention in 1863 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> made the city of San Luis Potosí the capital of the country. In 1866 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> line opened between San Luis Potosí and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, which helped begin the industrialisation of the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Besides the capital, major cities include Ciudad Valles, Matehuala, Rioverde and Tamazunchale. The area around Real de Catorce has some of the richest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines in the country, and Guadalcázar's ores yield gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, zinc and bismuth as well as silver. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> is the most widely spoken indigenous language, followed by Huasteco. The state's coat of arms shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> on a hill at Cerro de San Pedro, where the metals were found.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “San Luis Potosí” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century, raiders crossed the Rio Grande, wintered in the Bolsón de Mapimí and struck as far south as Durango, Zacatecas and <b>San Luis Potosí</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…about 1.7 million people speak Nahuatl, the largest number for any Indigenous language of Mexico, in the states of Puebla, Veracruz, Hidalgo, <b>San Luis Potosí</b>, Guerrero and others and in Mexico City; in 2003 a law recognised it and the other Indigenous languages as national languages of Mexico.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The Huastec separated from other Maya speakers well over two thousand years ago and settled the lowlands of northern Veracruz, <b>San Luis Potosí</b> and Tamaulipas.</blockquote><a href="/state/huastec-chiefdoms">Huastec chiefdoms</a></li></ul></section>
